                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> I suspect we can just enforce this at configure time?\n\nI'm not following either.\n\nUnrelated, this check has been added in several locations, and it could certainly be simplified with a helper function, which then only requires a single branch.\n\n```\nbool checkSingleHostSystemName(... asyncResp, const std::string& systemName) {\n\n    if constexpr (!BMCWEB_EXPERIMENTAL_REDFISH_MULTI_COMPUTER_SYSTEM)\n    {\n        if (systemName != BMCWEB_REDFISH_SYSTEM_URI_NAME)\n        {\n            messages::resourceNotFound(asyncResp->res, \"ComputerSystem\",\n                                       systemName);\n            return true;\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n```\n\nThen it simplifies to\n```\nif (checkSingleHostSystemName(asyncResp, systemName)){\n    return;\n}\n```\n\n@ed@tanous.net does that look good to you?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 910,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Oliver Brewka",
                        "email": "oliver.brewka@9elements.com",
                        "username": "mox669"
                    },
                    "message": "I have followed your suggestion and introduced the function in this patch [1]. \n\nMarking this as resolved. Feel free to unresolve if there is still sth we are overlooking.\n\n[1] https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/bmcweb/+/87159/1"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 943,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Gunnar Mills",
                        "email": "gunnar@gmills.xyz",
                        "username": "gtmills"
                    },
                    "message": "So you just don't add the link ? \nSo LogServices is just empty when MULTI_COMPUTER_SYSTEM ?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 943,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Oliver Brewka",
                        "email": "oliver.brewka@9elements.com",
                        "username": "mox669"
                    },
                    "message": "> So you just don't add the link ?\n\nCorrect. \n\n> So LogServices is just empty when MULTI_COMPUTER_SYSTEM ?\n\nIt contains the PostCodes LogService, for which the follow-up patch adds support for multi-host."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 952,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "See above, this one specifically, if these two options aren't allowed together, can we just make that enforced in meson?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 952,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Oliver Brewka",
                        "email": "oliver.brewka@9elements.com",
                        "username": "mox669"
                    },
                    "message": "For the log options I did solve this in meson. Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 974,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "systemName{std::move(systemName)}\n\nDoesn't really matter in practice, but static analyzers complain."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 974,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Oliver Brewka",
                        "email": "oliver.brewka@9elements.com",
                        "username": "mox669"
                    },
                    "message": "So do I need to change this? This is how we do it in multiple places apart from this patch\n\nAlso clang-tidy does not complain on my local machine, so I am a bit confused."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 974,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Also confused. Tried to look at the jenkins log but it seems to have already gone away.\n\n> but static analyzers complain.\n\nWhich one, which options ? Do we need to run anything besides tidy?\nI thought all our analyzers run in CI, and CI is green for this change."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 974,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Oliver Brewka",
                        "email": "oliver.brewka@9elements.com",
                        "username": "mox669"
                    },
                    "message": "A move on a const has no effect. Trying to do so my static analyzer actually starts to complain. Leaving as is."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 992,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This is now a security vulnerability as you've written it.  \"systemName\" comes from the user, so might contain bad characters (including query strings and other stuff).  BMCWEB_REDFISH_SYSTEM_URI_NAME comes from compile time, so std::format is appropriate, although really anything new should use boost::urls::format"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/lib/log_services.hpp",
                    "line": 992,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Oliver Brewka",
                        "email": "oliver.brewka@9elements.com",
                        "username": "mox669"
                    },
                    "message": "The follow-up patch already fixes that."
                }
            ],
